      "id": 1,
      "text": "In 1870, Phoebe and Holden Judson founded Lynden on Nooksack lands near the settlement of Squahalish.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynden%2C_Washington",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The 1893 post office was named Lynden after the Bavarian town of Hohenlinden, echoing Thomas Campbell’s poem.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynden%2C_Washington",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A road to Whatcom opened in 1875, and rail reached Lynden a decade later, linking it to regional trade.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynden%2C_Washington",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Since the 1980s, Lynden has celebrated Dutch roots with Holland Days and a Sinterklaas festival, supported by 28 Calvinist churches by 1995.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22,
        23,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lynden%2C_Washington",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Northwest Washington Fair, founded in 1909, remains an annual highlight of Lynden’s cultural calendar.",
